---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Launch from '@/components/motion/scenes/Launch.astro';
import { MotionSequenceReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-sequence.tsx';
import { MotionSequenceAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-sequence.ts';
import MotionSequenceSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-sequence.svelte';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="sequences"
  title="Motion: choreograph steps"
  description="Steps that play in order from one root, with one handle to play, pause and seek them. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <MotionStage scene="launch" label="Live example: Choreograph steps" height="lg">
    <Launch size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    A sequence plays the children of one root in steps, with one handle for the whole choreography. Each child that carries
    <code>data-motion-step="&lt;name&gt;"</code> belongs to the step of that name. The steps are defined once, from
    <code>@udixio/motion/sequence</code>. The example above has four: a kicker, a title, a sparkline and two stat cards.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`import { defineSequence } from '@udixio/motion/sequence';

defineSequence('intro', {
  verb: 'reveal',
  steps: {
    title: { keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, y: 24 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0 }] },
    subtitle: { keyframes: [{ opacity: 0 }, { opacity: 1 }], at: '+=normal' },
    actions: { keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, scale: 0.9 }, { opacity: 1, scale: 1 }], stagger: 'tight' },
  },
});`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>verb</code>: <code>appear</code> (the default) plays when the root is bound while inserted, or on <code>play()</code>. <code>reveal</code> plays when the root enters the view, and waits in the first frames of its steps until then.</li>
    <li><code>steps</code>: each named step has <code>keyframes</code> and, optionally, <code>timing</code>, <code>reduced</code>, <code>stagger</code> and <code>origin</code>, which mean what they mean for an appear motion. The elements of a step with <code>stagger</code> cascade in visual order.</li>
    <li><code>at</code> places a step on the timeline. Without <code>at</code>, the step starts when everything before it has ended. <code>at: '&lt;step&gt;'</code> starts it with an earlier step, and <code>at: '+=normal'</code> (or <code>tight</code>, <code>loose</code>) starts it that long after the previous step starts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>HTML and JavaScript</h2>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="intro">
  <h3 data-motion-step="title">…</h3>
  <p data-motion-step="subtitle">…</p>
</div>

import { sequence, getSequence } from '@udixio/motion/sequence';

const binding = sequence(panel, 'intro', { settled: true }); // bound, no entry yet
const handle = binding.play();   // plays the steps from the start
handle.pause();                  // holds them where they are
handle.seek(400);                // moves to 400 ms, forwards or backwards
await handle.finished;           // 'finished' or 'cancelled'

getSequence(panel)?.play();      // replays it`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    A finished handle is frozen: to seek it again, play it, pause it, then seek. <code>binding.handle</code> is the handle of
    the latest play, and <code>destroy()</code> releases the inline styles.
  </p>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    React <code>useMotionSequence('intro-steps')</code>, which returns a <code>ref</code> and <code>play()</code>; Angular
    <code>udxMotionSequence="intro-steps"</code>; Svelte <code>&#123;&#64;attach motionSequence('intro-steps')&#125;</code>.
    The examples replay their sequence with the button.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-sequence">
    <MotionSequenceReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionSequenceAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionSequenceSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'verb', values: 'appear · reveal', default: 'appear', description: 'When the sequence plays: when it is bound while inserted, or when its root enters the view.' },
      { name: 'steps', values: 'name: { keyframes, … }', description: 'The steps, by name. Each plays its keyframes on the children that carry its name.' },
      { name: 'at', values: 'an earlier step, or +=tight · +=normal · +=loose', description: 'When a step starts. Without it, the step starts when everything before it has ended.' },
      { name: 'stagger', values: 'tight · normal · loose', default: 'none', description: 'The step between the elements of one step, in visual order.' },
      { name: 'origin', values: 'first · last · center', default: 'first', description: 'Where the cascade of a step starts.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The launch example moves its title on the slow spatial spring, and draws its sparkline over the extra long curve. Its stat cards cascade loosely.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.slow.spatial', 'duration.extraLong2', 'easing.standard', 'stagger.loose']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>Under reduced motion every delay is 0, so the steps play together, each with its reduced render. The ruler still seeks the sequence.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A sequence is validated in development only, as a motion is.</li>
    <li>Within a step, the delays of a cascade are capped at <code>--udx-motion-stagger-max</code>, as for lists; the delays of <code>at</code> are not.</li>
    <li>A child that belongs to a nested sequence is not a step of the outer one.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
